The code statements that lead to the bug are:
memcpy(&data[1], &ipaddr->s6_addr[11], 4);
Reason:
The destination should be &data[2] and the source should be &ipaddr->s6_addr[12]. Using &data[1] overwrites the RIID field (data[1]) with s6_addr[11], corrupting the compressed multicast address. Additionally, data[5] is never written, leaving uninitialized kernel stack memory to be transmitted over the network, causing a kernel stack leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
u8 data[6] = {};
memcpy(&data[2], &ipaddr->s6_addr[12], 4);
Reason:
Zero-initializing the data array ensures no uninitialized bytes are transmitted. Changing the offsets to &data[2] and &ipaddr->s6_addr[12] correctly places the group ID bytes into data[2..5], matching the expected layout for decompression and preventing the corruption of the RIID field.

